{
 "coreName": "Eden",
 "systemId": "NSW",
 "systemNames": {
  "NSW": "Nintendo Switch"
 },
 "author": "The Eden, yuzu and Citra developers",
 "url": "https://github.com/mayonnaisegoodfellas/chimera-core-eden",
 "romFile": "game",
 "deterministic": true,
 "memoryLayoutMiB": [
  2048,
  16,
  256,
  256,
  24576
 ],
 "_memoryLayoutMiB_note": "sbrk, sealed, invisible, plain, mmap. The Switch's 4 GiB of RAM is one reservation on the mmap heap (only the pages the game touches cost anything), beside the page tables of a 39-bit address space, each core's JIT code cache, the renderer's shadows and the machine's own filesystem - the installed system software and what the game saved live in this memory, so a savestate carries what the game changed. Above 1x a 2 GiB block of address space on the mmap heap holds the pictures drawn at the larger size, which a savestate carries. The invisible pool, which no state carries, holds the picture the frontend takes (up to 5760x3240, twice) and the work lists for those pictures.",
 "video": {
  "_comment": "The console's own screen, 1280x720, drawn by Eden's OpenGL renderer through the GPU bridge; with Console Mode docked the picture is the television's, 1920x1080. Internal Resolution multiplies either, so width and height here are the largest picture there is (docked at 3x) and the picture's own size comes with each frame; the virtual size is the screen's shape. 60 Hz exactly: a frame is a sixtieth of a second of machine time.",
  "width": 5760,
  "height": 3240,
  "virtualWidth": 1280,
  "virtualHeight": 720,
  "vsyncNumerator": 60,
  "vsyncDenominator": 1,
  "getBgra": "GetVideoBgra",
  "gpuStatesSurviveTheContext": true,
  "_comment_gpuStates": "At every frame's end the renderer waits for the GPU and writes every picture and buffer it drew into the machine's memory, so a state holds them; when the GL context moves (a state loaded in this session or a later one) the renderer is made again and fills its caches from that memory."
 },
 "audio": {
  "_comment": "The audio renderer and audio out mixed into one 48 kHz stereo stream, pulled every 5 ms of machine time: a frame carries 720 or 960 pairs, 800 on average.",
  "rate": 48000,
  "samplesPerFrame": 2048,
  "channels": 2,
  "get": "GetAudio"
 },
 "input": {
  "name": "Nintendo Switch",
  "_comment": "Two Pro Controllers and the touch screen. A stick runs -32768..32767 with right and up positive. Touch X and Touch Y place one finger as a fraction of the screen, 0..65535 across and down; the Touch button puts it down.",
